{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Take for Me.</b> It should have said קח לי עגלה משולשת ('take for Me a three-year-old heifer'), or קח לך ('take for yourself'), as in קח לך מכל מאכל ('take for yourself of every food'), for this taking was for Avraham's benefit, to assure him of the gift of the land. But it is possible to interpret קחה לי as 'take for My Name,' that is, for the special Name." "<b>And already</b> the saying of our Rabbis of blessed memory is well known: in all the sacrifices, neither El nor Elohim nor 'your Elohim' is mentioned, but only the special Name — so as not to give an opening to the adversary to object. And so it is stated concerning sacrifices (Numbers 28:2): ״תשמרו להקריב לי במועדו״. And the added heh of קחה is like the added heh of המזבחה." "And you should already know that He is the recipient below and He is called 'righteousness' (צדק), and Scripture says (Deuteronomy 33:19): ״שם יזבחו זבחי צדק״. And the general principle of the matter is the heh at the beginning, and from there upward, ascent upon ascent until the supernal Cause; and this is the meaning of <b>Take for Me</b>. And it says ויקח לו ('and he took for himself') to indicate that he did as he was commanded and offered it to His Name, blessed be He."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "The comparison is to forms where a final heh is appended; המזבחה appears in Numbers 23:14 (ובנה שבעה מזבחות והקרב שור ואיל—some witnesses read המזבחה), the heh there being a directive/dative suffix."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:35.605219905Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}